description:
Scenario: handle_metadata_changes at metadata.rs:118-123:

  let max_changes: Option<u64> = match args.get("maxChanges") {
      None | Some(Value::Null) => None,
      Some(v) => Some(v.as_u64().filter(|&n| n > 0).ok_or_else(|| {
          JmapError::invalid_arguments("maxChanges must be a positive integer")
      })?),
  };

The filter(|&n| n > 0) rejects maxChanges=0 with invalidArguments. Good. But this is the metadata-server's COPY of the canonical jmap-server::handle_changes (handlers.rs:176-181). The duplication exists because handle_metadata_changes does its own arg-parsing to extract the filter args FIRST.

Maintenance consequence: if jmap-server's behavior for maxChanges parsing changes (e.g. cap to MAX_CHANGES_HARD_LIMIT), metadata-server's copy will drift. No comment, no DRY structure, no test confirming that metadata-server's parsing matches jmap-server's.

Fix direction: extract a shared maxChanges parser in jmap-server, use it from both handlers. The current 7-line duplication is small but is the kind of thing that adds up across 7 extension crates.
